        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "ea13816d4dbac266138a0be4124b636649d2284c",
            "parents": [
                "6b0f4f66bad4b940a34a51917173d8132ceb76c8"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/14/78014/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Christopher Sides",
                "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                "username": "csides-hpe"
            },
            "createdOn": 1738798956,
            "author": {
                "name": "Chris Sides",
                "email": "Christopher.Sides@hpe.com",
                "username": ""
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "What do you mean by this?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "In HPE's case, the devices in question were added to the bus from outside userspace by an HPE-specific I2C driver (i2c-gxp: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-gxp.c). \n\nSince the devices were added from outside userspace, it's my understanding that calls from inside userspace to 'delete_device' will fail.\n\nI\"ll think more about how to reword this line."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm no sure I follow. You've linked to the HPE GXP I2C bus driver, but I can't see that it's creating any sub-devices in its own right."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "Honestly, I'm not too personally familiar with the specific mechanism that's working to add devices to I2C bus from outside userspace (and don't have a bound driver for host-power race condition reasons) -- but the end result is that devices like '50-004c' are being added to i2c bus segments from outside userspace, and as a result, the usual \"add_device\" and \"destroy_device\" writes for those devices from userspace will be 'blocked.' That stops the devices from getting picked up by the usual daemons.\n\nI can do a bit more digging in for the specifics on where exactly that's happening, if that detail seems important to folks--\n\nbut I'd consider this to be a general \"this should work on any device added from outside userspace that had to have a driver to be bound to it by dbus daemons\" change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Well, I think it's important that we understand what the lifecycles at hand actually are if we're going to try fixing it?\n\nAre the devices described in your devicetree?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "The I2C bus segments are described in the DTS, but the individual sensors nodes that are discovered during the I2C-GXP probing process are not.\n\nMy current understanding of the life cycle is that HPE has published I2C-GXP drivers that scan each bus, detects sensor devices that may still be powered off, and adds the devices to the appropriate I2C bus, visible in sysfs. \n\nIt's my understanding that any device added to an I2C bus from outside userspace will not be able to make use of destroy_device or add_device (since a device already exists at the spot, and can't be removed from usespace) -- which is the main issue that stopped daemons like HwmonTempSensor from picking up sensors on HPE platforms.\n\nThis limitation would apply to any platform that has any devices added to an I2C bus from outside userpsace.\n\nI can do a bit more digging to work out \"where specifically does the device get added to a bus in GXP-I2C code\" element. I believe I had it figured in the past, but it's been long enough since I poked around in that code that I'll need to get a refresher.\n\nSidenote: I'm currently looking at putting together some code that would keep track of whether or not the daemon needed to bind a driver, which would determine whether or not it tries to unbind them when destroy() is called."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": ">detects sensor devices that may still be powered off, and adds the devices to the appropriate I2C bus, visible in sysfs.\n\nI asked someone with a deeper understanding of the existing HPE I2C stack, and am told it's not directly the GXP-I2C driver that's adding sensors after all.\n\n>The HPE GXP I2C driver provides the interface for the core I2C subsystem to interact with our I2C engine. The core i2c subsystem in collaboration with the device tree discovers devices listed there and will enable the probe of said devices when the I2c address is discovered.\n\nMy updated understanding is that device nodes visible in sysfs like '50-004c' are being added by a temp-sensor specific driver HPE has out that works on top of the structures enabled through the GXP-I2C driver. \n\nI'll keep digging in for more specifics here, but I don't expect the specifics to change the end result (add_device calls DBus Daemons rely on will fail and the sensor will be skipped), or the fix for it happening."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Sidenote: I'm currently looking at putting together some code that would keep track of whether or not the daemon needed to bind a driver, which would determine whether or not it tries to unbind them when destroy() is called.\n\nSounds good."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 200,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "The newest update now tracks sysfs paths for devices it binds drivers for.\n\nI've confirmed that 'destroy' works with the new checks, and successfully removes the appropriate path from the list of tracked devices when it's called.\n\nDestroy gets called if HwmonTempsensor does a write to 'probe' but the drive probe fails to create a hwmon node -- not uncommon, and is even expected -- happens early in boot due to host power race conditions."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 201,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "How do we know that there are not other users of the driver? Why do we want to unbind drivers that we didn't instantiate? Seems like a broken lifecycle."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 201,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "My thinking was that if we're at this point in the junction, it means we already tried to called destroy() on the object, which means we tried to do a 'delete_device' sysfs call -- which fails if the device in question was added to the bus from outside userspace.\n\nIn cases where a device was added to the bus from outside userspace (by a platform-specific I2C driver in HPE's case), but wasn't bound to a driver (for race condition reasons relating to host power), the dbus daemons will attempt to bind a driver (see 78011: DeviceMgmt: add driver binding support for existing devices | https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/78011 ).\n\nMy thinking was that if we're already trying to 'destroy' a device, it would *probably* be okay to just unbind the driver if that fails, especially since we have the ability to retry attempts to rebind that driver, especially on events like host power state changes. Restarting a d-bus daemon would also trigger a driver rebinding- if needed (but I wouldn't expect that to be necessary).\n\nThis PR calling to unbind drivers on destory()'s failure to write to delete_device is related to the one linked above that binds drivers in the first place-- but I don't think this function is not vital to that one, and figured it might generate enough discussion that it'd be worth having it as its own separate PR."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 201,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "clarification: this 'unbind' from destroy() functionalility is *not* vital to the 'bind drivers to existing devices' PR linked above -- but I feel like if we're adding the ability to bind drivers, it makes sense to allow it to unbind them too, assuming no one can think of anything specific this change would break."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/DeviceMgmt.cpp",
                    "line": 201,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "...maybe it'd make sense to have DeviceMgmt keep track of paths for anything it successfully did a driver binding for, then only unbind on delete_device fail if it's on that list.\n\nI'll do some experimenting there -- assuming no one chimes in to say it's not needed in the meantime..."
                }
            ],
